WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce-compile (90s) Narrator Lem

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.962
First, real compilers break any single descriptive law, so this study defines a seven-part yardstick instead.

2
00:00:07.462 --> 00:00:11.811
Second, maximum compiler optimization burns upfront energy that

3
00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:15.332
never pays back on thirty percent of test programs.

4
00:00:15.832 --> 00:00:21.320
Third, fingerprinting code by variable position reuses builds with zero mix-ups,

5
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.406
cutting Rust compile energy four hundredfold.

6
00:00:24.906 --> 00:00:30.756
We tested a sweeping claim—that every compiler lowers canonical forms while tracking energy

7
00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:37.120
and preserving meaning—against twelve real compiler families. In practice, none tracks joules, none

8
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.841
hashes name-free code, and automated fuzzers have uncovered thousands of C compiler bugs.

9
00:00:43.341 --> 00:00:48.849
Restating compilation as a seven-part standard lets us measure exact payback horizons

10
00:00:48.849 --> 00:00:54.098
across twenty kernels and fifteen toolchain tiers: optimized C beats Python after

11
00:00:54.098 --> 00:01:00.384
twenty-four hundred ninety runs, while shared Rust runtime libraries cut build energy twentyfold.

12
00:01:00.884 --> 00:01:07.710
Simply erasing variable names caused one hundred fifty dangerous cache collisions whenever two variables swapped

13
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:13.682
roles. Replacing names with De Bruijn binding positions hit zero false matches across five hundred

14
00:01:13.682 --> 00:01:18.984
verified mutants and cut warm C and Rust rebuilds up to four hundred forty-eight times.

15
00:01:19.484 --> 00:01:25.493
All energy figures come from built-in Linux processor counters rather than an external wall wattmeter,

16
00:01:25.493 --> 00:01:29.440
and every external citation carries an explicit verification badge.
